#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148590} :activity/id #uuid "57371a77-6667-5bdd-b857-9b52cc9a92ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048313 :topic/id #uuid "93cbb547-bfa6-5649-8003-160f883bcb4f" :topic/math-academy-id 2467} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148323 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147392 :question/id #uuid "4447e034-350f-5e6b-a501-0f57a3888bd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4196423-3401-54e1-b214-9dbc4861cfe1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147728} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233094}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Spheres, Hemispheres, Cones, and Cylinders"} :step/id #uuid "2317aeba-3862-5ebe-a106-96c1e216914d" :step/math-academy-id 33549 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147611 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147290 :question/id #uuid "319d252c-a8c4-5a5f-ad2b-f4bdb5702b75" :question/math-academy-id "e-2662"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8fd3c2c-3340-50df-a95c-3b82f165a071" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048698} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050585}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147899}] :knowledge-point/title "Naming Prisms"} :step/id #uuid "19df8c7e-89cb-5578-919d-65b12d903d26" :step/math-academy-id 8908 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147612 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147291 :question/id #uuid "0583c82a-049c-51e6-aebc-bcd32423824e" :question/math-academy-id "e-2668"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9d83422-a877-5de8-bc55-2c7aae9eeb72" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147915}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Polyhedra"} :step/id #uuid "a01f473c-01ac-5a25-a94d-294c2cc662a2" :step/math-academy-id 8914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148714 :tutorial/content "A **polyhedron** is a $3$-dimensional shape where each side is a polygon. These flat polygonal sides are called **faces**.\n\n**Prisms** are polyhedrons made of two identical polygonal faces (called **bases**) connected by equal parallel line segments. If these line segments are perpendicular to the bases, we have a **right** prism.\n\nLet's take a look at some common prisms.\n\n- A **triangular (right) prism** consists of two identical triangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments.\n\n![](images/95/9563bd4f9c40ff5921998ed5840f8e5b43fb1af579a64a06a14782be4a86ece2.png)\n\n- A **rectangular (right) prism** consists of two identical rectangular faces connected by equal parallel line segments. Notice that each face is a rectangle.\n\n![](images/39/39ea3dd56722c7fb05ba275a054ff46479e3ee96851dafdd66fe09455a517f1a.png)\n\n- A **cube** is a special case of a rectangular prism that consists of six equal square faces.\n\n![](images/41/415d03a9a886793eee4b4d16e50af26002c0881279481b2e70773b8b7bff02a5.png)\n\nBy adding more sides to the polygonal base, we can make more prisms. For example, the following shape is a pentagonal prism.\n\n![](images/8f/8f4dd8fe8c120eabbe5a1ad6413f6f0cd9cd3d22efe8b13e25ac3d2a916f3258.png)\n\nWe can also construct hexagonal prisms, heptagonal prisms, etc." :tutorial/id #uuid "af52fff0-b45a-59d7-85f8-cacc3a9cb9f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2202 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a54ee23c-aa2f-55da-8cdb-fd96e504ff67" :step/math-academy-id 8915 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148718 :tutorial/content "**Pyramids** are polyhedrons made of a polygonal base, with the other triangular faces meeting at a single point.\n\nLet's take a look at some common pyramids.\n\n- A **triangular pyramid** has a triangular base with $3$ other triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/d8/d8597d1e526e2af72255f7dce1cc13e01cf76f1c53522003b126be67a0a6c55d.png)\n\n- A **rectangular pyramid** has a rectangular base with $4$ triangular faces that meet at a single point. If the base is a square, we call this a **square pyramid.**\n\n![](images/26/26700acaf9eae64227988ddcfb43269c0eba6f6447229ec8854cc1ebfeab3334.png)\n\n- A **pentagonal pyramid** has a pentagonal base with $5$ triangular faces that meet at a single point.\n\n![](images/4c/4c73b984b3e4a2ccb0796646ebb4c547947fab7359914599d928a63819818725.png)\n\nSimilarly to prisms, we can add more sides to the polygonal base to make more types of pyramids." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ebd5b00-1afa-5ee5-9321-47a3e76e71ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2248 :tutorial/title "Pyramids"} :step/id #uuid "0e005ac2-7765-5ed0-83c6-dc038809de33" :step/math-academy-id 9198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147294 :question/id #uuid "dc558592-e65f-5369-890d-fffc633f2a3b" :question/math-academy-id "e-2757"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ddd0beef-ff0c-5ef3-88c4-ce49fbb8d5cb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147900}] :knowledge-point/title "Naming Pyramids"} :step/id #uuid "4c62acce-9aa5-51e7-aab3-ddef11ded75b" :step/math-academy-id 9199 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148719 :tutorial/content "Not all three-dimensional shapes are polyhedrons. Let's see some examples.\n\n- A **sphere** is the set of points with the same distance to a fixed point called the **center.** A sphere is a $3$-dimensional analog of a circle. A **hemisphere** is half of a sphere.\n\n![](images/84/84e7b63b5ec8761d4a3ac8a00b15a08f0dab7d4f95a536721df0c82e2999873f.png)\n\n- A **cylinder** is a solid shape with two parallel circular bases. There are two types of cylinders.A cylinder is **right** if the axis that passes through the centers of the circular bases is perpendicular to the bases. Otherwise, the cylinder is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/f8/f8ddb161d2d8628dd1294cc0d0042437300787217b518be73f010e79be90dcf7.png)\n\n- A **cone** is a solid shape with one circular base and one vertex called the **apex**. There are two types of cones.A cone is **right** if the axis that passes through the center of the circular base and the vertex is perpendicular to the base. Otherwise, the cone is **oblique.**\n\n![](images/2f/2f9e9ca18a36336ed99695bb51b167ffbd83f002139728f8dfe15227bf9f7745.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "daf27141-b818-5e08-b09c-b940a0ab763a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2250 :tutorial/title "Other Three-Dimensional Shapes"} :step/id #uuid "9570e433-348e-5b8c-8ac5-b5e75693bf4e" :step/math-academy-id 9201 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148323}}] :activity/title "Identifying Three-Dimensional Shapes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147667}]}
